## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## feature_gap_fill  (v2 — one agent closes the gap in its natural
##                    flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a GAP-CLOSING campaign: the input is a STRUCTURED gap
## spec ("here is what's implemented, here is what's missing" — typically
## authored by the adr-cartograph bot as a `type:feature-gap` issue). The
## campaign reads the partial implementation the spec references, builds a
## living todo from the `missing` items, and completes ONLY those — one
## item at a time, attached to the existing seams, committing each item in
## stride. It preserves what already works; it does NOT re-architect.
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` / `branch_improve_loop` v2: SAME shape
## (one adaptive `campaign` agent + a deterministic build/test gate + a
## bounded continuation loop + git-as-state), DIFFERENT scope — the gap
## spec defines the work. No PR tail: Fini answers a board issue whose
## commits land on the run worktree's storage branch; feature_dev owns the
## issue→PR lineage.
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 gap loop was ~12 nodes of staged machinery
## (survey_existing → plan → act → simplify → alt → reviewer_claude /
## reviewer_gpt → streak_check → fix_claude / fix_gpt → prepare_commit →
## commit_changes). It OVER-FRAMED the work — the same lesson
## whole_improve_loop learned (ADR-058,
## docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is
## framing, not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs itself". A
## capable agent closes a well-specified gap better as ONE flow — survey
## the seams, a living todo of missing items, one verified commit per
## item — than as an assembly line of survey/plan/act/review/fix/commit
## nodes.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools). Mission =
##                  the GAP SPEC + a standing-autonomy contract that
##                  reproduces the productive-session shape AGENT-SIDE: a
##                  brief read-only survey of the implemented surfaces
##                  (the seams), a LIVING todo built from the `missing`
##                  items, then the repeated unit locate seam → smallest
##                  change → build → test → semantic COMMIT, one item at a
##                  time. git IS the durable state — a re-dispatch re-runs
##                  `campaign`, which reads `git log` + re-checks the spec
##                  and continues. Termination output: {gap_closed,
##                  commits_this_pass, missing_remaining, needs_human, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` skill
##     + verify_run  and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
##                  <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node
##                  re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code (no LLM
##                  judgment). The tight real-feedback loop AND the
##                  anti-Goodhart truth oracle: the agent can't self-certify.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND gap_closed → converged
##                  → done; else loop back to `campaign` (RED → with the
##                  failure log so the agent fixes what it broke; green but
##                  items remain → another pass). No LLM, no shell.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## item in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked every
## committed item (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign
## commits the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER its own build+test passes,
## staging untracked files (`git add -A`) so an item that ADDS files (the
## common case for a gap: new tests, new helpers) actually lands; the
## deterministic verify gate then re-checks the committed tree.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the gap spec + the repo define the work. No
## language / package-manager literal, and no iterion-specific target
## path, appears in any var default, command body, or schema.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): survey_existing (absorbed
## into the campaign's opening survey), plan / act / simplify (the
## session-inherit chain), the `alt` round_robin router,
## reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt, the streak_check convergence compute,
## fix_claude / fix_gpt, prepare_commit / commit_changes, and the
## existing_state / verdict / streak / fix / commit schemas. The campaign
## self-reviews via a fresh re-check of the spec + the deterministic
## build/test gate + its own judgment (the operator's proven manual
## pattern), and commits each item in stride. 12 nodes → 5.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## gap_spec is a STRUCTURED description of what's already implemented
  ## vs what is still missing. Typically authored by the adr-cartograph
  ## (Adry) bot as the body of a `type:feature-gap` issue, but operators
  ## can also pass it directly via `--var gap_spec='...'`. The expected
  ## shape (loose — Fini parses prose, not a strict JSON schema):
  ##   - implemented: files / abstractions already in place
  ##   - missing:     the concrete deliverables Fini must add
  ##   - evidence:    references (paths, line numbers) anchoring the survey
  ## REQUIRED — no default; a run without a gap spec has nothing to close.
  gap_spec: string

  ## Free-form extra context for the campaign agent (e.g. constraints the
  ## issue thread surfaced, priorities among the missing items). Empty =
  ## the gap spec speaks for itself.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY. The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to
  ## ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox (never
  ## hardcode ~/.iterion here — the -full image's pinned non-host User
  ## cannot write a host-owned path → EACCES). Keeping it OUT of the
  ## target worktree is the repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone
  ## else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/feature-gap-fill"

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't its own. Empty (default) = the
  ## campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check) then
  ## skips what predates its work. The dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass
  ## is one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked)
  ## when the gap is reported closed + the tree is green, or when this cap
  ## is hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop. Raise it (with the
  ## budget) for a wide gap; the agent commits in stride, so every pass
  ## banks real, landed work.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── Plan phase ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ## The plan phase itself — the AUTHORED plan the campaign implements
  ## from — runs by default, on every deployment, whatever `plan_review`
  ## resolves to. off = skip planning altogether: the campaign starts
  ## immediately and plans in stride (the deliberate cost-saving posture
  ## for a small, well-specified gap). Independent of `plan_review`,
  ## which gates ONLY the cross-model peer review of that plan.
  plan_phase: string [enum: "on", "off"] = "on"
  ## ─── Cross-model pair review of the plan ──────────────────────
  ## Gates ONLY the peer review — never the plan phase (plan_phase).
  ## Resolved at LAUNCH by pkg/reviewtopology from the run's
  ## credentials: auto → "on" iff a SECOND model family is credentialed
  ## (host detection locally, the sealed bundle on cloud), else "off" —
  ## the plan is then authored and handed to the campaign unreviewed,
  ## stamped as such, at zero extra cost. "on" forces the review (the
  ## peer reviewer then fails loudly on a missing credential).
  plan_review: string = "auto"
  ## What a MID-RUN peer failure (forfait window shut, provider down,
  ## credential revoked) does:
  ##   skip (default) — the reviewer's `action: skip` fallback route
  ##     completes it with a zero-value critique stamped _skipped; the
  ##     plan proceeds unreviewed, LOUDLY (plan_gate reads the stamp).
  ##     The peer is an optional enrichment: the primary family alone
  ##     must always suffice, so a dead peer credential never parks the
  ##     whole campaign.
  ##   wait — the failure stays a failure: the run parks
  ##     failed_resumable and the run-level usage-window retry resumes
  ##     it when the window reopens — the deliberate-spend posture.
  plan_review_policy: string [enum: "wait", "skip"] = "skip"

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior engineer CLOSING a precisely-specified GAP on an
  existing partial implementation — working EXACTLY as you would in a
  productive, human-driven engineering session. You are NOT building from
  zero. You have the repo in front of you and full tools (read, edit,
  shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass end to end.

  THE SCOPE is the GAP SPEC (in your user prompt). It lists what is
  already implemented (PRESERVE it) and what is missing (DELIVER exactly
  that). Spec drift is common — the spec may overstate or understate the
  current state, so verify against the source and trust the code over the
  spec when they disagree; when the drift changes the work materially,
  say so in `summary`. Do NOT widen the scope to unrelated parts of the
  workspace.

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session, agent-side:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Start with a BRIEF read-only
     survey: open the files the spec marks as implemented, verify what is
     actually there, and identify the architectural seams the missing
     parts will attach to — the existing types, functions, packages,
     naming conventions, error-handling style. Then build a todo list
     from the spec's `missing` items cross-referenced against those
     seams. Keep it DENSE and MOVING — re-prioritize, append, and defer
     items as you learn. Do NOT write 5–6 coarse upfront phases and tick
     them: the list is a metronome born from the survey, not a plan
     frozen before it.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one missing item at a time: locate the exact seam
     it attaches to → make the SMALLEST change that closes the item there
     (EXTEND an existing abstraction whenever possible; ADD a new
     file/symbol only when no seam fits — never introduce a parallel
     abstraction when an existing one can be reused) → build → run the
     tests → COMMIT it with a semantic message (`feat(scope): <precise
     item>`, or the `type(scope):` that fits — `test(...)` for added
     tests). Every commit message carries the trailer line
     `Bot: feature-gap-fill`; when the gap issue id is known (see
     scope_notes), also add `Refs: <id>`. A few edits per commit,
     validation BEFORE the commit — never commit code you have not built
     and tested green.

  3. COMMIT EACH ITEM AS YOU FINISH IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`, so an item that ADDS files — the
     common case for a gap: new tests, new helpers — actually lands) and
     commit before moving on. git IS your durable state: an interrupted
     run keeps every item you committed, and a fresh pass just reads
     `git log`, re-checks the spec, and continues where you left off. Do
     NOT keep a separate worklist / cursor file — the todo list lives in
     your working memory, the commits are the record.
     END EVERY PASS ON A CLEAN TREE: if you must stop mid-item, either
     finish it to committable or discard the in-progress edits — `git
     restore --staged --worktree -- <the paths you touched>` and
     delete any new files you created; never a blanket `git clean`
     (the workspace may be the operator's own checkout). The
     deterministic gate verifies the WORKING TREE, and a half-done
     item (an un-vendored dependency, a dangling import) reds the gate
     as noise.

  4. PRESERVATION DISCIPLINE — the gap spec's `implemented` items are
     load-bearing. Do NOT touch them unless directly required to wire up
     a missing part; when a missing part DOES force a change there,
     prefer the minimal extension that wires the new code through (add a
     parameter, expose a hook, register a handler) over a rewrite.
     Re-architecting working code is out of scope. If you find yourself
     wanting to refactor or clean up pre-existing code outside the gap,
     STOP and capture it as a board finding instead (see FINDINGS
     HANDOFF below).

  5. BASELINE — don't chase failures you didn't cause. Pre-existing test
     failures / known-flaky tests are in your user prompt (`baseline`).
     Do NOT try to fix or investigate those; if a test was already red
     before your change, SKIP it and note it. If the baseline is empty,
     do ONE cheap `git stash`-based check to establish which failures
     pre-date your work, then stop investigating them. Don't burn budget
     proving a failure isn't yours.

  6. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. You MAY pause for the operator (the
     ask-user tool) on a genuine decision you cannot make on your own —
     the spec contradicts the code in a way that changes the deliverable,
     a missing item admits two incompatible designs, a public contract
     would have to break. Keep it RARE: most of the time you decide and
     keep moving.

  ADR-AUTHORING IS OUT OF SCOPE: the adr-cartograph (Adry) bot owns ADR
  drafting for this lineage. Do NOT create or update files under the
  repo's ADR directory here — note ADR-worthy decisions in `summary` so
  the next Adry run picks them up.

  FINDINGS HANDOFF — after your last commit, capture anything you noticed
  that is OUT OF SCOPE for this gap but worth tracking (a pre-existing
  bug, a doc drift, a refactor opportunity, an infrastructure issue):
  call `mcp__iterion_board__list_issues` with `{ "state": "inbox" }`
  first to avoid duplicates, then `mcp__iterion_board__create_issue` per
  NEW finding with state "inbox" and labels ["findings", "kind:<bug|
  drift|improvement|question>", "source:feature_gap_fill",
  "area:<subsystem>", "severity:<low|medium|high>"]. Be conservative —
  zero findings is a fine outcome; do not manufacture findings to look
  productive. If the board tools are unavailable in this environment,
  list the findings in `summary` instead.

  KEEP GOING, one item at a time, until this pass has closed every
  missing item you can within your budget. Then STOP and report.

  PERSISTENCE — the durable path, to the end. Never declare a
  sub-problem infeasible on reasoning alone: instrument it (add debug
  output, shrink the repro, bisect) and let the evidence decide — most
  premature verdicts of infeasibility are one experiment away from a
  small fix. Prefer the durable path over the expedient shortcut: no
  façade that satisfies the letter of the gate, no silently skipped or
  weakened test, no temporary patch where the real fix is reachable
  this pass. If something genuinely stays out of reach, bank your
  state first — commit what is green, then a precise `missing_remaining`
  note naming the paths you tried and the evidence that closed them —
  never a silent abandon.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the
  engine detects completion; an under-specified end just gets re-poked):
    - gap_closed: true ONLY when a fresh re-check of the gap spec finds
      EVERY `missing` item implemented, integrated and tested. false if
      items remain (a later pass continues from your commits).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - missing_remaining: a concise note of which missing items still need
      work (empty when gap_closed).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator
      decision.
    - summary: what you delivered, which seams you attached it to, any
      spec drift you found, and any ADR-worthy decisions for Adry.

  Be honest. A DETERMINISTIC build/test gate re-checks the tree after you
  and the run loops you back for another pass until you report gap_closed
  AND the tree is green — so under-reporting only costs a pass, and
  over-reporting lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  GAP SPEC (what's implemented — preserve it — vs what's missing —
  deliver exactly that):
  {{vars.gap_spec}}

  Extra context / notes (empty = the spec speaks for itself):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures / known-flaky tests to SKIP (empty =
  establish it once cheaply, then skip what predates your work):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first
  pass; when present, the build/test tree was RED after your last pass —
  FIX exactly what this shows before continuing the gap):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  IMPLEMENTATION PLAN (empty = no plan phase ran, plan in stride as
  usual; on continuation passes read `git log` instead). Provenance —
  who authored it and whether a cross-model peer challenged it (empty
  = no plan):
  {{input.plan_provenance}}
  The plan is a MAP, not a contract — reality wins; note where you
  diverge:
  {{input.plan}}

  Peer critique of that plan (cross-model reviewer; the plan author
  already challenged it and integrated what held — weigh what remains
  on its merits, empty = none or the review was skipped):
  {{input.plan_critique}}

  Plan author's responses to the critique (accepted/rejected and why):
  {{input.plan_responses}}

  Close the gap one missing item at a time — locate the seam → smallest
  change → build → test → commit — until a fresh re-check of the spec
  finds nothing missing. Read `git log` first to see what earlier passes
  already landed. Then report the termination contract (gap_closed,
  commits_this_pass, missing_remaining).

## ─── supervisor (Persy, the perseverance coach) ─────────────────────
## Concurrent LLM watcher on the campaign node enforcing the contract's
## PERSISTENCE clause from outside. Monitors are pre-seeded (armed from
## the run's first event); see docs/supervisors.md for the knobs and the
## per-run kill switch. Every text marker is pinned to `assistant_text`
## — the agent's own words — so tool output and prompt echoes never wake
## an eval (measured: 5 of 10 evals burned on echoes when unpinned).
supervisor persy:
  watches: [campaign]
  system: persy_policy
  cooldown: "5m"
  max_evals: 10
  monitors: ["event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=impossible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=unsolvable", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=infeasib", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=not feasible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=give up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=giving up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=gave up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=workaround", "event_type=budget_warning", "event_type=budget_exceeded"]

prompt persy_policy:
  You are Persy, a perseverance coach watching a senior engineer agent
  (the `campaign` node) close a structured gap spec — the missing items
  of a feature — end to end. Your ONLY job is to keep it on the durable
  path and moving to completion — the push a good human operator
  supplies when watching the session live. The agent's own contract
  already states the policy (its PERSISTENCE clause); you enforce it
  from outside when the agent drifts.

  STAY SILENT while progress is steady — exploration, edits, passing
  builds, commits landing. Silence is your default; most wakes end
  with intervene: false. Your monitors are pre-seeded; register more
  only when the run shows you a sharper signal.

  INTERVENE with ONE short message (2–4 sentences, specific and
  actionable, like an operator typing mid-session) only on:
  1. GIVING UP — a sub-problem declared hopeless, or quietly
     skipped/reported away. Demand one more INSTRUMENTED attempt (add
     debug output, shrink the repro, bisect) and name the cheapest
     next experiment visible in the recent events. Evidence decides,
     not fatigue.
  2. EXPEDIENT PATH — a façade, a skipped or weakened test, a
     temporary patch where the durable fix is reachable this pass.
     Name the durable alternative and ask for it.
  3. FAILURE LOOP — the same command failing repeatedly with no change
     of approach. Ask for a different approach, not another retry.
  4. PRESSURE — a budget warning or a visibly long pass. Tell it to
     BANK NOW: commit what is green, write a precise missing_remaining,
     leave the tree clean — before attempting anything else.

  NEVER: contest an honest termination report — gap_closed, or a
  precise missing_remaining with evidence, is a valid outcome
  (completion is the goal, not perpetual motion); enlarge the scope
  beyond the gap spec; re-raise a point the agent already answered
  with evidence; stack a second message on a signal you already
  steered. Once the agent reports its structured termination output,
  you are done.

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC
## tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code. No
## language / package manager is named here — the gate stays universal while
## the verdict stays deterministic. verify_build does NOT fix code: a RED gate
## routes back to `campaign` (the one capable agent) to fix what it broke.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A gap-closing pass just applied changes to
  this repository and committed them. Your single job: capture how THIS repo
  builds and tests itself into a re-runnable script the deterministic gate
  will execute. You do NOT change code.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain (a task runner / Makefile target, a
     package-manager test script, or a language build+test command),
     HONOURING any pinned toolchain (version managers, lockfiles, devbox,
     GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path, NOT inside the repo — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build AND its tests
     (or the closest fast equivalent) and exits non-zero on any failure. Keep
     it to the commands a developer of THIS repo would run; no network-heavy
     or destructive steps.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: some repos are already red on the base tree,
     before this run touched anything. Scope `verify.sh` to the build plus the
     test targets the campaign's changes actually affect (package/module
     level), so the gate measures THIS run's work and a red base can't absorb
     it — and list any excluded pre-existing failures in your summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid (the commands resolve).
     You do NOT fix code here: if the build is RED because the campaign broke
     something, the deterministic gate reports it and the campaign fixes it on
     the next pass. Just make `verify.sh` faithfully run the repo's real
     build+test.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config), write
     a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its real
  exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` exists
  and reflects the repo's real build+test; summary = the command you settled
  on and any pre-existing failures you scoped out.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build + test into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  (an out-of-tree scratch path — see the system prompt + the `verify-build`
  skill). Do NOT change code; the deterministic gate runs your script and the
  campaign fixes any red on the next pass.

  MANDATORY (skill section 1b): if the repo's own CI enforces a
  codegen-drift gate (git diff --exit-code after a regen/vendor step, a
  drift-check task, test -z on git status --porcelain), verify.sh MUST
  mirror it — run the repo's regen command(s), then fail on drift. The
  deterministic precheck rejects a gateless verify.sh in that case
  (exit 3) and the loop burns a full pass re-learning this.

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the deterministic gate's failure log from the PREVIOUS
## pass (empty on the first pass and on a green-but-more-work continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  fail_log: string
  ## Plan-phase hand-off (empty when the phase is off / skipped / on
  ## continuation passes — git is the durable state there).
  plan: string
  plan_critique: string
  plan_responses: string
  ## Who authored the plan and whether a cross-model peer challenged it:
  ## a literal stamp set on each hand-off edge, empty when no plan ran.
  plan_provenance: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the engine
## keys convergence on. gap_closed is the done-oracle (a fresh re-check of the
## gap spec finds every missing item delivered); the rest is observability.
schema campaign_output:
  gap_closed: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  missing_remaining: string
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is set from
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT an
## LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was produced
## (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = the tree is
## green AND the campaign reported the gap fully closed. fail_log relays the
## real build failure to the campaign on a RED pass (empty when green).
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## ─── Plan phase (authored plan + opt-in cross-model pair review) ───
## The plan AUTHOR (claude family, read-only) maps the work; then, ONLY
## when `plan_review` resolved "on" at launch, a peer from the OTHER
## family (claw + gpt) critiques it against the actual repo and the SAME
## author session challenges the critique and integrates what holds;
## then the campaign implements. ONE revision turn, no loop — the
## campaign stays the arbiter of reality (asymptote intact). With the
## review off, the campaign still gets the authored plan, stamped
## unreviewed. `plan_phase: off` skips the phase altogether.

prompt plan_system:
  You are the implementation planner for a gap-fill campaign (a structured
  spec of missing or incomplete items to deliver). You are
  READ-ONLY: explore the repository and produce a PLAN — change nothing.

  Explore briefly (the implementing agent re-verifies reality): the
  files involved, the existing patterns to REUSE, the seams the work
  attaches to. Then write the plan as a MAP, not a contract — the
  implementer may diverge where reality disagrees.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the implementation plan — the slices in order (a slice = a
      coherent, buildable, testable increment), each naming the
      files/seams it touches and what it reuses.
    - assumptions: the load-bearing assumptions the plan rests on.
    - risks: where the plan is most likely wrong, and what to check.

prompt plan_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  THE GAP SPEC to plan the fill for:
  {{vars.gap_spec}}

  Scope notes (advisory bounds):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

prompt plan_review_system:
  You are a peer reviewer from a DIFFERENT model family, adversarially
  reviewing an implementation plan against the actual repository. Your
  value is INDEPENDENT judgment: verify the plan's claims in the code
  with your read-only tools; do not extend the plan's own reasoning.

  Hunt for: a wrong or missing assumption about the code; a slice that
  builds a parallel abstraction where an existing seam fits; an
  integration or test gap the plan does not cover; a materially simpler
  shape; an understated risk.

  Structured output:
    - concerns: the defects you can GROUND in the repo (name the
      file/seam per claim). Empty when the plan holds — do not
      manufacture concerns to look useful.
    - suggestions: concrete improvements, most valuable first.
    - blocking: true ONLY for a defect that would make the delivered items
      wrong (not merely improvable).

prompt plan_review_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  THE GAP SPEC the plan fills:
  {{vars.gap_spec}}

  Scope notes (advisory bounds):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  THE PLAN under review:
  {{input.plan}}

  Its stated assumptions:
  {{input.assumptions}}

  Its stated risks:
  {{input.risks}}

prompt plan_revise_system:
  You authored the implementation plan earlier in this session. A peer
  reviewer from another model family has critiqued it. CHALLENGE the
  critique: verify each concern against the repository, integrate what
  holds, refuse what does not — with a stated reason. You revise the
  PLAN only; you change no code.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the final plan, revised where the critique held.
    - review_responses: one line per concern — accepted (and how the
      plan changed) or rejected (and why).

prompt plan_revise_user:
  Peer critique of your plan:
  {{input.concerns}}

  Peer suggestions:
  {{input.suggestions}}

  Blocking: {{input.blocking}}

  Your plan as reviewed:
  {{input.plan}}

## workspace_probe output — the deterministic "is there a repository to
## work on?" verdict. ok=false carries the typed code WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO
## and a reason naming what was refused.
schema workspace_probe_state:
  ok: bool
  code: string
  reason: string

schema plan_topology_state:
  do_plan: bool

schema plan_review_topology_state:
  do_review: bool

schema plan_output:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string

schema plan_review_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string

schema plan_review_output:
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

schema plan_gate_state:
  skipped: bool

schema plan_revise_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

schema plan_revised_output:
  plan: string
  review_responses: string

## workspace_probe — DETERMINISTIC precondition and the run's ENTRY: a
## launch with nothing to work on is refused BEFORE any LLM node spends
## (a `--bot` launch carrying only pr_url attaches no repository; the
## plan node would otherwise author a plan against nothing). Refuses
## with the typed code WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO when workspace_dir is absent
## or is not a git repository (`git rev-parse --git-dir` fails). The
## verdict is the node's OUTPUT (ok/code/reason, routed to the named `workspace_not_a_repo` fail node, which stamps that code on the RUN) and is echoed on stderr for the tool log; the process exits
## 0 on purpose — a non-zero exit would replace the typed verdict with
## the engine's generic tool failure. python3 for safe JSON (mirrors
## verify_run); read-only, ~100ms.
tool workspace_probe:
  description: "Precondition: workspace_dir is a git repository"
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json, sys
ws = (os.environ.get('WS') or '').strip()
def refuse(reason):
    sys.stderr.write('WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO: ' + reason + chr(10))
    print(json.dumps({'ok': False, 'code': 'WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO', 'reason': reason}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
if not ws or not os.path.isdir(ws):
    refuse('workspace_dir ' + repr(ws) + ' does not exist')
p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'rev-parse', '--git-dir'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, timeout=60)
if p.returncode != 0:
    refuse('workspace_dir ' + repr(ws) + ' is not a git repository (git rev-parse --git-dir: ' + (p.stderr or '').strip()[-300:] + ')')
print(json.dumps({'ok': True, 'code': '', 'reason': 'git repository at ' + ws}))
"`
  output: workspace_probe_state

## workspace_not_a_repo — the entry precondition refused. NOT resumable:
## nothing about this run changes by resuming it, because the workspace it
## was launched against is not the thing the operator can widen. Fix the
## launch (attach a repository) and start again.
fail workspace_not_a_repo:
  description: "the launch has no repository to work on"
  code: WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO
  message: "{{outputs.workspace_probe.reason}}"
  resumable: false

## plan_topology lifts the plan_phase switch into a bool so the entry
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM. This gate is the
## PHASE (on by default); whether a cross-model peer reviews the authored
## plan is decided after it exists, by plan_review_topology.
compute plan_topology:
  description: "Plan phase gate (plan_phase switch)"
  output: plan_topology_state
  expr:
    do_plan: "vars.plan_phase == 'on'"

## plan — the AUTHOR. Same family/model as the campaign (the peer is the
## cross-check). readonly is enforced on codex/pi only — on claude_code
## (bypassPermissions) it is intent, not a guarantee; the deterministic
## verify gate + the campaign's own review remain the backstop.
agent plan:
  description: "Author the implementation plan (read-only)"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  output: plan_output
  system: plan_system
  user: plan_user
  session: fresh
  readonly: true

## plan_review_topology — is a cross-model peer wired for this run? Lifts
## the launch-resolved plan_review var (auto → on iff a second model
## family is credentialed) into a bool AFTER the plan is authored, so an
## edge condition can read it. off → the authored plan goes to the
## campaign unreviewed, stamped so the campaign knows no peer looked at
## it. No LLM.
compute plan_review_topology:
  description: "Peer review gate (launch-resolved plan_review)"
  output: plan_review_topology_state
  expr:
    do_review: "vars.plan_review == 'on'"

## plan_review — the PEER, pinned to the other family. claw because
## "openai/*" models only resolve there; both knobs env-overridable so a
## host without OpenAI quota can run the peer elsewhere. Read-only
## toolset (no write_file/file_edit tools — though bash can write; the
## restriction is intent + review-pr's shipped posture, not a sandbox).
judge plan_review:
  description: "Peer plan review (other model family)"
  backend: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_BACKEND_GPT:-claw}"
  model: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-5.6-sol}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"
  input: plan_review_input
  output: plan_review_output
  system: plan_review_system
  user: plan_review_user
  session: fresh
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  fallbacks:
    ## The plan_review_policy switch, expressed as a route gate:
    ## policy "skip" arms this terminal route — a peer failure completes
    ## the node with a zero-value critique stamped _skipped (plan_gate
    ## routes around the revise turn). policy "wait" leaves NO active
    ## route: the failure stays failed_resumable and the run-level
    ## usage-window retry parks the run until the forfait reopens.
    ## on: [any] — deliberately unfiltered: the operator's `skip` means
    ## the peer must NEVER block the campaign, whatever killed it. It is
    ## also load-bearing under the shipped `sandbox: auto`: a sandboxed
    ## claw failure flattens to a string at the IPC boundary and
    ## classifies UNCLASSIFIED, which a filtered skip refuses by design.
    give_up:
      action: skip
      when: "vars.plan_review_policy == 'skip'"
      on: [any]

## plan_gate — did the peer actually serve, or did the skip route fire?
## Deterministic: reads the _skipped stamp, never an LLM judgment.
compute plan_gate:
  description: "Peer review served or skipped?"
  output: plan_gate_state
  expr:
    skipped: "outputs.plan_review._skipped == true"

## plan_revise — the AUTHOR again, SAME session (the `_session_id`
## carried on the edge), challenging the peer's critique. Read-only.
agent plan_revise:
  description: "Author challenges & integrates the critique"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: plan_revise_input
  output: plan_revised_output
  system: plan_revise_system
  user: plan_revise_user
  session: inherit_if_available
  readonly: true

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset (read/edit/shell/git) so it works exactly like a native Claude
## Code session: a brief survey of the seams, a living todo of missing
## items, one verified commit per item, in stride. session: fresh — a fresh
## pass re-derives its todo from the spec + `git log` (git is the durable
## state). reasoning_effort high, not max: the productive-session data says
## the working SHAPE, not the model tier, is the lever (framing >
## capability). interaction: human — the agent may pause for the operator
## on a genuine mid-flight decision (kept rare by the contract). Board
## capabilities power the end-of-pass findings handoff (out-of-scope
## observations → inbox), replacing v1's prepare_commit handoff.
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human
  capabilities: [board.read, board.create]

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a per-item
## session. high, not max: mechanical capture of the repo's build/test.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. No language /
## package manager appears here — it executes whatever verify_build wrote, so
## the gate stays universal while the verdict stays deterministic. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def sh_uncommented(text):
    # The script and the CI config, read with comments removed -- shared by
    # every structural check below so a commented-out gate never counts as
    # one and a commented-out pipeline never accuses.
    out_lines = []
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        cut = len(raw)
        i = 0
        while i < len(raw):
            if raw[i] == chr(35) and (i == 0 or raw[i - 1] in (chr(32), chr(9))):
                cut = i
                break
            i = i + 1
        out_lines.append(raw[:cut])
    return out_lines
def ends_nonzero(s):
    # A statement that ENDS the script non-zero: exit/return with a non-zero
    # (or inherited) status, or a bare false.
    for kw in ('exit ', 'return '):
        p = s.find(kw)
        while p >= 0:
            rest = s[p + len(kw):].lstrip()
            if rest[:1] in ('1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8', '9') or rest[:1] == chr(36):
                return True
            p = s.find(kw, p + 1)
    return s == 'false' or s.endswith(' false') or s.endswith(';false')
def tree_state():
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
    except Exception:
        return None
pre = tree_state()
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
# Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
# deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
# CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
# must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
if rc == 0:
    import glob
    def has_drift_gate(text):
        # STRUCTURAL detection: a drift gate is a drift PROBE whose failure ENDS
        # the script. Read one line at a time the commonest shape was invisible --
        # the exit 1 of an  if ! git diff --quiet ...; then  sits on a LATER line
        # -- so a genuinely green verify was refused with DRIFT GATE MISSING.
        # Comments are stripped first: a comment can DESCRIBE a gate, only a
        # command can BE one.
        lines = sh_uncommented(text)
        fail_fast = False
        for i, l in enumerate(lines):
            s = l.strip()
            if s.startswith('set -'):
                flags = (s.split() + [''])[1]
                if 'e' in flags[1:] or 'errexit' in s:
                    fail_fast = True
            probe = ('diff' in l and ('--exit-code' in l or '--quiet' in l)) or ('status --porcelain' in l)
            if not probe:
                continue
            # 1. --exit-code IS the gate: the command's own status is the verdict.
            if '--exit-code' in l:
                return True
            # 2. the verdict on the SAME line.
            if ('exit 1' in l) or ('|| exit' in l) or ('|| return' in l) or ('test -z' in l) or ('[ -z' in l):
                return True
            # 3. NEGATED conditional -- if ! <probe>; then ... exit 1 ... fi. The
            #    branch runs WHEN THE TREE DRIFTED, so a non-zero exit anywhere in
            #    it is the gate; scan to the matching else/fi. A body that COMMITS
            #    the drift instead (the updater shape) never reaches an exit and
            #    is still correctly rejected.
            if (s.startswith('if ') or s.startswith('elif ')) and '!' in s.split(';')[0]:
                depth = 0
                j = i + 1
                while j < len(lines):
                    b = lines[j].strip()
                    if b == 'fi' or b.startswith('fi '):
                        if depth == 0:
                            break
                        depth = depth - 1
                    elif depth == 0 and (b == 'else' or b.startswith('elif ')):
                        break
                    elif b.startswith('if ') or b == 'if':
                        depth = depth + 1
                    if ends_nonzero(b):
                        return True
                    j = j + 1
                continue
            # 4. FAIL-FAST script -- a bare  git diff --quiet  under set -e aborts
            #    on drift, so the mode itself is the gate. Restricted to the diff
            #    form on purpose: git status --porcelain exits 0 with output, so a
            #    bare one gates nothing. Never a condition, a captured value or a
            #    swallowed status.
            if fail_fast and 'diff' in l and '--quiet' in l:
                bare = not (s.startswith('if ') or s.startswith('elif ') or s.startswith('while ')
                            or s.startswith('until ') or s.startswith('!'))
                if bare and '||' not in s and '&&' not in s and (chr(36) + '(') not in s:
                    return True
        return False
    ci_hit = ''
    for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
        for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
            try:
                if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                    ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                    break
            except OSError:
                pass
        if ci_hit:
            break
    if ci_hit:
        try:
            vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
        except OSError:
            vs = ''
        if not has_drift_gate(vs):
            rc = 3
            out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
    if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
        post = tree_state()
        if post is not None:
            fresh = sorted(post - pre)
            if fresh:
                rc = 4
                out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. Reads the real gate result
## (outputs.verify_run.passed) AND the campaign's termination output
## (outputs.campaign.gap_closed), both fresh in the loop BODY (the same
## discipline whole_improve_loop's gate uses to read the loop-head safely).
## converged = green AND the gap is fully closed. On a RED pass fail_log
## carries the real build failure to the campaign; empty when green. No LLM,
## no shell.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.campaign.gap_closed"
    fail_log: "if(outputs.verify_run.passed, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail)"

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow feature_gap_fill:
  ## Entry is the deterministic workspace precondition: a launch with no
  ## repository to work on fails typed (WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO) before any
  ## LLM node spends. Then the plan phase: on by default, the plan is
  ## AUTHORED, peer-reviewed cross-model ONLY when `plan_review` resolved
  ## on at launch (a second model family credentialed), and handed to the
  ## campaign — stamped with its provenance — before it implements.
  ## `plan_phase: off` starts the campaign immediately (plan in stride).
  ## A re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log` and continues from what
  ## earlier passes banked either way.
  entry: workspace_probe

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/. The bot
  ## mutates code AND commits; the worktree + finalize FF guarantees: (a) the
  ## operator's main checkout is untouched during the run, (b) on convergence
  ## the commits land on a persistent branch `iterion/run/<friendly-name>` AND
  ## fast-forward the operator's currently-checked-out branch (best-effort,
  ## skipped with a warning on conflict).
  worktree: auto

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): the platform sandboxes by default
  ## (`sandbox: auto` — target repo's devcontainer, else the default
  ## image); the target repo's toolchain comes from its own
  ## devcontainer/devbox.json.

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h30m"
    max_cost_usd: 75

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Precondition: nothing LLM runs on a workspace with no repository.
  workspace_probe -> plan_topology when ok
  workspace_probe -> workspace_not_a_repo when not ok

  ## Plan phase: on by default; plan_phase off → straight to the campaign.
  plan_topology -> plan when do_plan
  ## Every campaign_input field is mapped EXPLICITLY empty: an unmapped
  ## field is not "" but the raw {{input.x}} placeholder leaking into
  ## the prompt (the engine keeps unresolved refs as-is).
  plan_topology -> campaign when not do_plan with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: ""
  }

  ## The authored plan meets the peer-review gate: a second model family
  ## credentialed (plan_review on) → cross-model critique + author
  ## revision; otherwise the plan reaches the campaign unreviewed, with a
  ## provenance stamp that says so.
  plan -> plan_review_topology
  plan_review_topology -> plan_review when do_review with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}"
  }
  plan_review_topology -> campaign when not do_review with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node - NOT peer-reviewed (plan_review resolved off: one model family credentialed, or the operator said off); the author's own map, unchallenged"
  }
  plan_review -> plan_gate
  ## Peer served → the plan AUTHOR (same session) challenges the
  ## critique and integrates what holds; skipped (policy skip + peer
  ## unavailable) → the unreviewed plan proceeds, loudly.
  plan_gate -> plan_revise when not skipped with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}",
    concerns:    "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    suggestions: "{{outputs.plan_review.suggestions}}",
    blocking:    "{{outputs.plan_review.blocking}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.plan._session_id}}",
    _session_fingerprint: "{{outputs.plan._session_fingerprint}}"
  }
  plan_gate -> campaign when skipped with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node - the cross-model peer review was SKIPPED mid-run (peer unavailable, plan_review_policy skip); the author's own map, unchallenged"
  }
  plan_revise -> campaign with {
    fail_log:        "",
    plan:            "{{outputs.plan_revise.plan}}",
    plan_critique:   "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    plan_responses:  "{{outputs.plan_revise.review_responses}}",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node, critiqued by a cross-model peer, and revised by the author (its accepted/rejected responses follow)"
  }

  ## Each pass: the campaign closes missing items (committing each in
  ## stride), then the deterministic build/test gate re-checks the tree.
  campaign -> verify_build
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged (green AND the campaign reports the gap fully closed) → done.
  gate -> done when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log so the
  ## agent fixes what it broke; green-but-more-work carries an empty log so the
  ## agent simply continues the gap. This closes the single declared loop
  ## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign); the loop-back reads
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log (a BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own
  ## output on its back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  ## The back-edge BLANKS the plan-phase fields: at loop re-entry the
  ## back-edge is an OVERLAY applied last — forward-edge mappings from
  ## sources with output still contribute the unmapped keys — so a
  ## stale pass-1 plan would re-anchor later passes that must read
  ## `git log` instead.
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}",
    plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "", plan_provenance: ""
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while items still remain) →
  ## finish anyway, shipping what is banked (every committed item is real,
  ## landed work; the last campaign_output's missing_remaining tells the
  ## operator what is left). Unconditional fallthrough after the loop-back
  ## retries are spent.
  gate -> done
